Inside the request body, the attacker uses GraphQL aliasing to pack 5,000 distinct `setValveState` mutations into this single HTTP transaction. Because the WAF only counts HTTP requests, it registers this as "1 request" and permits it. The backend Apollo router, however, must resolve all 5,000 mutations, resulting in severe processing latency (8.4 seconds) and initiating a massive, concurrent fan-out of physical shut-off commands to the neighborhood concentrators.

